const intro={"n":5,"title":"Abuse at the Frontier and a Violent Misunderstanding","summary":"Fierro describes military service as a trap rather than meaningful defense. He accuses officers of using soldiers as laborers for their own estates. Desperate to escape, he recalls an incident at night: a foreign-born sentry, drunk and unable to recognize him, challenges him; Fierro answers mockingly, the sentry fires, and Fierro is subsequently tied down and punished. The rest of the canto contains Fierro's contemptuous generalizations about foreign-born recruits. Those insults belong to a historically situated character and should not be accepted as descriptions of immigrants.","people":"Martín Fierro; his frontier commanders; an immigrant sentry who identifies himself as 'papolitano'; the officers who order Fierro's punishment.","themes":"Corruption and coerced labor; military punishment; misunderstandings and language; prejudice against migrants; the distinction between victimhood and unreliable generalization.","words":"estaquiada (punishment tying or stretching a person to stakes); centinela (sentry); gringo (a term for a foreigner, used disparagingly here); fortín (frontier outpost); pingo (horse); peludo (drunkenness in this passage).","questions":["What evidence does Fierro give that soldiers are being used for officers' private work?","How do drinking, language and fear contribute to the sentry's confrontation with Fierro?","Why does the incident lead Fierro to resent immigrant recruits? Is his conclusion justified by the incident?","How should a student discuss the offensive language without adopting its prejudice?"],"nums":[135,145,147],"analysis":"The canto does more than complain about hardship: it stages a frightening mistake and the punitive machinery that follows. Fierro is vulnerable to officers, yet he also directs sweeping insults at other vulnerable men. A responsible reading can acknowledge both experiences. The text does not establish that the foreign recruits are all incompetent, nor should it be quoted as evidence about a whole community.","related":"/en/magazine/frontier-forts/","relatedTitle":"Historical frontier forts"};
